//? Title: Custom Operation Kernels: Raw Loops on the Tape
//? Subtitle: An operation's definition, kernel and gradient, the rounding error of summation, memory-bound fusion and deterministic parallelism, derived, measured and applied to a fused logistic loss on breast-cancer data, using datamunge's nn::Function and nn/kernel.hpp
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A custom operation is the lowest level at which a model can be changed: a loop over raw memory
that the autograd tape treats as one node. Writing one means deciding things the library otherwise decides.
Which dtypes are allowed? What shapes are valid? How is work split across threads? How are numbers
added? What gradient goes with the result? This report builds two operations, an elementwise scale-and-shift and a row sum, from new
library helpers for dtype dispatch, a parallel map and summation, and checks their gradients to second order. The theory
covers three things that decide a kernel's quality. Summation error: in float32, naive summation of \(10^7\) uniform values is off by \(6.7\times10^{-5}\) relative, close to
the typical \(\sqrt n\,u\) and far inside the worst-case bound \(nu\). Pairwise, Kahan and double-accumulator summation all return the correctly
rounded sum. Memory traffic: a fused one-pass kernel runs at the speed of a copy and is about twice as fast as the same
arithmetic composed from two operations. Determinism: an elementwise kernel is bit-identical at any thread count, while splitting a naive
float32 sum of \(1.7\times10^7\) values into chunks moves the answer by up to 10 442 units in the last place. Finally, a fused binary cross-entropy kernel for ridge-penalised
logistic regression on the breast-cancer data cuts the tape from 19 operations to 9. It gives the same coefficients as the
composed loss to \(2\times10^{-16}\) and matches scikit-learn to the printed four decimals. The library gained `nn::dispatch_floating`,
`nn::map_elements`, `nn::sum_with`, `nn::Summation` and `nn::summation_error_bound` (in `nn/kernel.hpp`).
)

\toc

> The problem

The previous report in this group (`custom_gradient`) wrote custom backward functions on top of `nn`'s own
operations. Sometimes the forward pass itself must be new code: a function the library lacks, a fused
version of several operations, or a call into hand-tuned code. Such a *kernel* reads and writes raw
memory, so the library's guarantees no longer apply automatically. The kernel must check dtypes and shapes,
handle strided views, use threads safely, add numbers accurately, and supply its own gradient.

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.1_tensors_graphs_sessions/custom_op_kernel.cpp`,
a port of TensorFlow's custom-op tutorial. TensorFlow splits an op into a definition (`REGISTER_OP`, with attributes and a
shape function), kernels per device and dtype (`REGISTER_KERNEL_BUILDER`) and a gradient (`REGISTER_GRADIENT_OP`), all registered by
static initialisers. `nn` has no registry. An operation is an `nn::Function` built at run time, which maps the TensorFlow parts as follows:

- the attributes become values captured when the function is built;
- the shape function becomes a check at the top of the kernel, which runs eagerly, so build time and run time coincide;
- the per-dtype kernel becomes one template dispatched on the dtype;
- the gradient becomes the function's backward.

> The method

>> Anatomy of an operation

An operation \(y=\phi(x;\theta)\) with attributes \(\theta\) needs four parts.

1. *Definition and validation.* Which dtypes and ranks are accepted, and the output's shape as a function of the input's. A
   rank-2 input with shape \([r,c]\) gives a row sum of shape \([r]\). Errors are raised before any memory is touched.
2. *Packing.* A tensor may be a strided view (a transpose, a slice). The simplest correct kernel calls `contiguous()`
   first, which copies only when needed, and then indexes \(x[i\cdot c+j]\).
3. *The loop*, written once as a template over the element type `T` and instantiated by `nn::dispatch_floating(dtype, …)` for float and double.
   `nn::map_elements(x, f)` packages the elementwise case: pack, allocate, and call `f` on chunks of the flat range through `nn::parallel_for`.
4. *The gradient*, a vector-Jacobian product as in the previous report. For scale-and-shift, \(\bar x=s\,\bar y\). For the row sum,
   \(y_i=\sum_jx_{ij}\) gives \(\partial y_i/\partial x_{ij}=1\), so \(\bar x_{ij}=\bar y_i\): each row's adjoint is copied along the row (`broadcast_to`).

>> Rounding error of summation

Floating-point addition rounds: \(\mathrm{fl}(a+b)=(a+b)(1+\delta)\) with \(\lvert\delta\rvert\le u\), the unit roundoff, \(2^{-24}\approx5.96\times10^{-8}\) in float32 and
\(2^{-53}\) in float64. Adding \(x_1,\dots,x_n\) left to right, the term \(x_i\) passes through \(n-i+1\) roundings (\(x_1\) through \(n-1\)). Multiplying out the factors
\((1+\delta)\) gives the classical bound (Higham, 2002, §4.2)

$$
\lvert\hat s_{\text{naive}}-s\rvert\le\gamma_{n-1}\sum_i\lvert x_i\rvert,\qquad\gamma_k=\frac{ku}{1-ku}\approx ku .
$$
\alttext(The absolute error of the naive sum is at most gamma n minus 1 times the sum of the absolute values of the terms, where gamma k equals k u over 1 minus k u, about k u.)

The bound is linear in \(n\). If the rounding errors behave like independent random signs, their sum grows only like \(\sqrt n\,u\), the
typical case. Three remedies change the constant:

- *Pairwise* summation adds the two halves recursively. Each term then passes through about \(\log_2n\) roundings, so the bound is \(\gamma_{\lceil\log_2n\rceil}\).
  The library version sums blocks of 8 naively, which adds 7.
- *Kahan's* compensated summation carries the part of each addition that was rounded away, \(c=(t-s)-y\), into the next term. Its bound
  \(2u+O(nu^2)\) is independent of \(n\) until \(n\approx1/u\).
- A *wide accumulator* adds float32 values in double and rounds once at the end. Its error is \(u\) from the final rounding, plus
  \(\gamma_{n-1}\) at \(u=2^{-53}\), which is negligible below \(n\approx10^{9}\). That is what `nn::sum` does for float32.

`nn::sum_with(values, n, method)` implements the four methods and `nn::summation_error_bound(method, n, dtype)` returns the factor
multiplying \(\sum\lvert x_i\rvert\) in each bound.

>> Memory-bound kernels and fusion

An elementwise kernel does a few arithmetic operations per element, against 8 bytes moved for a float32 read and write. On current
hardware that is far below the arithmetic a core can sustain per byte of memory bandwidth (the "roofline", Williams, Waterman and Patterson, 2009), so for arrays larger
than the caches the running time is

$$
T\approx\frac{\text{bytes moved}}{\text{bandwidth}} .
$$
\alttext(The time T is about the number of bytes moved divided by the memory bandwidth.)

Computing \(2x+0.5\) as two operations reads \(x\), writes a temporary, reads it and writes the result: 16 bytes per element. One fused pass moves
8. The prediction is that the fused kernel runs at the speed of a plain copy and the composed version takes twice as long, whatever
the arithmetic.

>> Determinism under parallelism

`nn::parallel_for` splits a range into chunks that may run on different threads. An elementwise kernel writes each output from its own input,
so the result is bit-for-bit independent of the number of chunks. A reduction is not: floating-point addition is not associative,
and splitting a sum into \(k\) partial sums changes the order of the roundings. A reproducible reduction must therefore fix its order:
`nn::sum` gives each output row to one thread and accumulates that row in a fixed order. The RowSums kernel below does the same, and splits
across rows, never within one.

> Simple examples

>> ScaleShift and RowSums

The program builds `ScaleShift(x; s, b)` = \(sx+b\) on `nn::map_elements`, and `RowSums(x; method)` with the summation method as
an attribute and a parallel loop over rows. It then uses both on \(x=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\4&5&6\end{pmatrix}\) and checks a composition of them with `gradcheck` and `gradgradcheck`.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using Tensors = std::vector<Tensor>;

// ScaleShift(x; scale, shift) = scale x + shift, any floating dtype, elementwise.
nn::Function scale_shift(double scale, double shift) {
  return nn::Function(
      "ScaleShift",
      [=](const Tensors& in) {
        return Tensors{nn::map_elements(in[0], [=](auto v) { return v * static_cast<decltype(v)>(scale) + static_cast<decltype(v)>(shift); })};
      },
      [=](const Tensors&, const Tensors&, const Tensors& g) { return Tensors{g[0] * scale}; });
}

// RowSums(x; method): the sum of each row of a matrix, with the summation method as an attribute.
nn::Function row_sums(nn::Summation method) {
  return nn::Function(
      "RowSums",
      [=](const Tensors& in) {
        const Tensor& x = in[0];
        if (x.dim() != 2)  // the shape function: rank 2 in, [rows] out
          throw std::invalid_argument("RowSums: expected a matrix, got shape " + nn::shape_str(x.shape()));
        const Tensor packed = x.contiguous();
        const std::int64_t rows = x.shape()[0], cols = x.shape()[1];
        Tensor out = nn::empty({rows}, x.dtype());
        nn::dispatch_floating(x.dtype(), "RowSums", [&](auto tag) {
          using T = decltype(tag);
          const T* src = packed.data_ptr<T>();
          T* dst = out.data_ptr<T>();
          nn::parallel_for(0, rows, 64, [&](std::int64_t lo, std::int64_t hi) {
            for (std::int64_t i = lo; i < hi; ++i) dst[i] = nn::sum_with(src + i * cols, cols, method);
          });
          return 0;
        });
        return Tensors{out};
      },
      // d(row sum i)/d x_ij = 1: the adjoint of row i is copied across that row.
      [](const Tensors& in, const Tensors&, const Tensors& g) {
        return Tensors{nn::broadcast_to(nn::expand_dims(g[0], 1), in[0].shape())};
      });
}

int main() {
  auto x = nn::tensor({{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}}).requires_grad_();  // float32
  const Tensor y = scale_shift(2.0, 0.5)(x);
  const Tensor yd = scale_shift(0.5, -1.0)(nn::cast(x.detach(), nn::DType::Float64));
  const Tensor s = row_sums(nn::Summation::Kahan)(y);
  std::print("- ScaleShift(x; 2, 0.5) = {} ({})\n", y.values_str(), nn::dtype_name(y.dtype()));
  std::print("- ScaleShift(x; 0.5, -1) in float64 = {} ({})\n", yd.values_str(), nn::dtype_name(yd.dtype()));
  std::print("- RowSums(ScaleShift(x; 2, 0.5)) = {}\n", s.values_str());
  std::print("- d sum(y²)/dx = {} (2 y · scale)\n", nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::square(y)), {x})[0].values_str());
  std::print("- d sum(RowSums(x)²)/dx = {} (2 · row sum, copied along each row)\n",
             nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::square(row_sums(nn::Summation::Naive)(x))), {x})[0].values_str());

  const auto f = [](const Tensors& in) { return nn::sum(nn::square(row_sums(nn::Summation::Pairwise)(scale_shift(2.0, 0.5)(nn::square(in[0]))))); };
  const Tensor x64 = nn::cast(x.detach(), nn::DType::Float64).requires_grad_();
  const auto r1 = nn::gradcheck(f, {x64}), r2 = nn::gradgradcheck(f, {x64});
  std::print("- gradcheck of sum(RowSums(ScaleShift(x²))²): ok = {}, error {:.1e}; gradgradcheck: ok = {}, error {:.1e}\n", r1.ok,
             r1.max_abs_error, r2.ok, r2.max_abs_error);
  const auto t = nn::tape({f({x64})}, {{x64, "x"}});
  std::print("\n```\n{}```\n\n", t.to_text());

  for (auto&& [label, call] : std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::function<void()>>>{
           {"RowSums of a vector", [] { (void)row_sums(nn::Summation::Naive)(nn::tensor({1, 2, 3})); }},
           {"ScaleShift of int32", [] { (void)scale_shift(1, 0)(nn::tensor({1, 2}, {2}, nn::DType::Int32)); }}}) {
    try {
      call();
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
      std::print("- {}: `{}`\n", label, e.what());
    }
  }
}
```
