\caption(The tape of one evaluation of the penalised loss. Left: with the fused kernel, one FusedBCE node sits between the logits and the final sum. Right: composed from library operations, the same loss records nineteen operations, including the selects, exponential and log1p of the stable softplus.)
\alttext(Left: a graph of nine operation nodes; from Add_11 at the top, one branch goes through Mul_10, Sum_9 and Square_8 to the leaf w, the other through FusedBCE_7, Add_6 (joined to the leaf b), Reshape_4, BatchMatMulV2_3 and Reshape_2 to w. Right: a deeper graph with nineteen operation nodes in eleven rows above the leaves b and w, with Add_34 at the top and nodes named Div, Sum, Add, Log1p, Exp, SelectV2, Neg and Mul in the middle.)

The fused loss records 9 operations per evaluation instead of 19, so each training step saves ten tape nodes, their saved tensors and their backward
calls. After 3000 steps both versions reach the same coefficients to \(1.7\times10^{-16}\), the rounding of the last bit, because they compute the same
gradient. The gradient norm, \(7\times10^{-7}\), shows the minimum has been reached. The penalised loss 0.099591, the intercept \(-0.4953\) and the six largest
coefficients agree with scikit-learn's `LogisticRegression(C = 1/(nλ))` on the same standardised data to all printed digits. The fit classifies
98.6% of the training tumours correctly, and the largest coefficients belong to the "worst" (largest) texture, radius and concave points, and to the
variability of the radius. On this small problem the fused version took roughly a third less time. On larger models, where the loss is one operation among thousands,
the saving matters less than the memory not spent on intermediate tensors.

> Conclusion

A custom kernel is a loop over packed memory wrapped in an `nn::Function`. It has the four parts of a TensorFlow op: validation,
packing, a loop templated on the dtype, and a gradient that is a vector-Jacobian product. `nn::dispatch_floating` and `nn::map_elements` remove the repetitive
parts, and an elementwise kernel written with them is thread-count independent and runs at memory bandwidth. Three
measurements set the design rules. Elementwise kernels are limited by bytes moved, so fusing two passes into one halved the time. Summation in
the element type loses accuracy like \(\sqrt n\,u\), so a float32 reduction should accumulate in double or use pairwise or Kahan summation, each of which returned the
correctly rounded sum of \(10^7\) terms. And a reduction split differently across threads gives different answers, by thousands of units in the last place, so
parallel reductions should split over independent outputs, not within one.

The fused logistic loss showed what fusion buys and what it does not. The tape halved and the step was faster, with identical results. The
gradient, however, had to be derived and checked by hand, and the kernel accepts only what it was written for. A custom operation is worth
writing when a profile shows the composed version is the bottleneck, or when the library lacks the operation entirely.

> References

- N. J. Higham (2002). *Accuracy and Stability of Numerical Algorithms*, 2nd edition, chapter 4 (Summation). SIAM. doi:10.1137/1.9780898718027
- W. Kahan (1965). "Further remarks on reducing truncation errors". *Communications of the ACM* 8(1), 40. doi:10.1145/363707.363723
- S. Williams, A. Waterman and D. Patterson (2009). "Roofline: an insightful visual performance model for multicore architectures". *Communications of the ACM* 52(4), 65–76. doi:10.1145/1498765.1498785
- J. Demmel and H. D. Nguyen (2013). "Fast reproducible floating-point summation". *IEEE Symposium on Computer Arithmetic*, 163–172. doi:10.1109/ARITH.2013.9
- M. Abadi et al. (2016). "TensorFlow: a system for large-scale machine learning". *OSDI*, 265–283.
- W. H. Wolberg, W. N. Street and O. L. Mangasarian (1995). "Breast cancer Wisconsin (diagnostic)". UCI Machine Learning Repository. doi:10.24432/C5DW2B
- F. Pedregosa et al. (2011). "Scikit-learn: machine learning in Python". *Journal of Machine Learning Research* 12, 2825–2830.
